  "formal_question": "A square with perimeter $48 \\mathrm{~cm}$ is cut into two equally big pieces with one cut. They are fitted together to make a rectangle as shown in the diagram. How big is the perimeter of that rectangle?\nChoices:\nA:$24 \\mathrm{~cm}$\nB:$30 \\mathrm{~cm}$\nC:$48 \\mathrm{~cm}$\nD:$60 \\mathrm{~cm}$\nE:$72 \\mathrm{~cm}$",
  "material_difference": "Old exposed_cut_edge_migration selected the horizontal12cm CUT and its two exposed12cm copies and targeted adding24 without removing12; presetE, actualD60/D60 remains MISS. New design removes ALL those marks and selects a different original feature: the UNCUT left exterior12cm side, now split between a6cm outer end and a6cm hidden contact. The distinct misconception transfers the full original unit12 to EACH half, giving rectangle width12 and length24, hence72=E. No old observed error is reused (old outputs were60). New source-to-result ownership and scale mapping is substantive despite the same target letter.",
  "primary_unique_math_proof": "The square side is48/4=12. The actual horizontal midpoint cut gives two12x6 congruent rectangles. In the shown end-to-end assembly the long side is24 and short side6, so perimeter2(24+6)=60, D uniquely. Boundary accounting independently gives48+2*12-2*6=60. For an intrinsic-ink interpretation, translate the lower source half by(7.5,1) and upper half by(11.5,-1) in display coordinates. Source left lower edge [0,0]..[0,2] maps to x7.5,y1..3 exterior, and upper edge [0,2]..[0,4] maps to x11.5,y1..3 seam. Both red halves are conserved consistently; red does not require them each to be12. The source cut itself and new exposed cut edges are black. Dashed seam remains interior under either material or comparison reading.",
  "human_semantics": "This is physical cutting and rearrangement but a numerical perimeter question. Unlike Plane242, the exact selected ink has a consistent rigid-piece migration shown explicitly in the proof; no colored feature disappears from an exposed boundary or creates a second feasible rectangle. The contact remains dashed. Same color is no equality glyph; 12 versus6 follows the visible cut.",
